"Feature layers—ArcGIS Online Help | Documentation (https://doc.arcgis.com/en/arcgis-online/reference/feature-layers.htm)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doc.arcgis.com/en/arcgis-online/reference/feature-layers.htm\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 133\nL0: [Input: Search ArcGIS Online Help] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL1: \nL2: cite0†Back to Top L3: \nL4: # Feature layers\nL5: #### In this topic\nL6: \nL7:   1. cite1†Layers in feature layers L8:   2. cite2†Hosted feature layers L9:   3. cite3†ArcGIS Server feature layers L10:   4. cite4†Feature collections L11:   5. cite5†Feature layer functionality L12: A feature layer is a grouping of similar geographic features, for example, buildings, parcels, cities, roads, and earthquake epicenters. Features can be points, lines, or polygons (areas). Feature layers are most appropriate for visualizing data on top of basemaps. You can set properties for feature layers—such as cite6†style , cite7†transparency , cite7†visible range , and cite8†refresh interval —that control how the layer appears in the map.\nL13: Using a feature layer, you can view, edit, analyze, and run queries against features and their attributes.\nL14: Each type of feature layer meets a different need and, therefore, has slightly different functionality. For a comparison of functionality available with each type of feature layer, see cite5†Feature layer functionality .\nL15: \nL16: For information on Open Geospatial Consortium (OGC) features, see cite9†OGC .\nL17: ## Layers in feature layers\nL18: \nL19: Each feature layer item can contain one or more layers. These layers (also called sublayers) can each store different geometry types; for example, a parks feature layer item can contain a point layer representing trees, a line layer representing trails, and a polygon layer representing park buildings.\nL20: Sublayers can also represent different types of data, such as a cite10†catalog layer or an cite11†oriented imagery layer . You can add sublayers individually to a map if you do not need every layer in the feature layer item.\nL21: \nL22: There are some settings, such as enabling attachments, that are always set on the sublayer. Other settings, such as metadata, can be different for a sublayer than for the feature layer item.\nL23: ## Hosted feature layers\nL24: \nL25: Hosted feature layers are layers that have been cite12†published to ArcGIS Online. The feature data in these layers is hosted by, or stored on, ArcGIS Online. These layers offer the most flexibility, scalability, and compatibility across ArcGIS. In addition to the general feature layer functionality cite13†listed above , you can do the following with hosted feature layers:\nL26:   * Edit data in the field either online or offline using apps such as ArcGIS Survey123 or ArcGIS Field Maps.\nL27:   * Share locations from mobile apps such as ArcGIS Field Maps.\nL28:   * Control editor tracking and feature layer cite14†capabilities and settings .\nL29:   * cite15†Create views from the feature layer to support different user needs.\nL30:   * cite16†Publish tile layers to support high-demand use cases.\nL31:   * cite17†Publish WFS layers to support requirements for OGC-compliant layers.\nL32:   * cite18†Publish 3D layers from feature layers containing points or multipatch layers.\nL33: You can publish hosted feature layers through a variety of workflows, including ready-to-use cite19†templates .\nL34: ### Hosted Indoors Spaces feature layers\nL35: \nL36: When you publish a cite20†Reservations layer†pro.arcgis.com that conforms to the ArcGIS Indoors information model to an ArcGIS Online organization that is licensed for Indoors Spaces, a hosted Indoors Spaces feature layer is created. Hosted Indoors Spaces feature layers are used to enable cite21†workspace reservations in Indoors Viewer and the Indoors mobile apps.\nL37: Hosted Indoors Spaces feature layers have similar functionality to other hosted feature layers when you interact with them in ArcGIS Online, with the following exceptions:\nL38: \nL39:   * Anyone who has access to an Indoors Spaces feature layer can edit it.\nL40:   * You cannot share Indoors Spaces feature layers with the public, distributed collaboration groups, or groups that allow external users.\nL41: ## ArcGIS Server feature layers\nL42: \nL43: If you host layers on an ArcGIS Server site, you can use these cite22†ArcGIS Server feature services as layers in ArcGIS Online. These layers are similar to hosted feature layers with the following differences:\nL44:   * The data is not copied to ArcGIS Online; it stays in the data source you registered with the ArcGIS Server site.\nL45:   * When you delete the ArcGIS Server feature layer, the data is not deleted.\nL46:   * Much of the configuration for ArcGIS Server feature layers is performed outside of ArcGIS Online.\nL47:   * cite5†Functionality on the ArcGIS Online website is different for ArcGIS Server feature layers than for hosted feature layers.\nL48: Many of these differences guard against unwanted changes being made to the data stored in the folders and databases you registered with the ArcGIS Server site, as this data is often your system of record and is likely accessed directly by other applications. However, the hosted feature layer data is managed by ArcGIS Online and not directly accessed by other applications.\nL49: If you want to work with an ArcGIS Server feature layer in ArcGIS Online, you can cite23†add the layer from the ArcGIS Server site to ArcGIS Online from My content. This allows you to reference the REST endpoint (URL) of the service and use the layer in maps and apps, while leaving the data stored in the data sources you registered with the ArcGIS Server site. You can also cite24†add ArcGIS Server feature layers to Map Viewer .\nL50: ### Layers in an ArcGIS Server map or feature service\nL51: \nL52: You can add individual layers in a map service or feature service as an item or to a map in Map Viewer. This allows you to use a single layer in the service in a map or app instead of having to use all the layers in the service.\nL53: For example, if you have access to an ArcGIS Server map service that contains a roads layer, a buildings layer, and a railroads layer, but you only need to access the roads, you can add the roads layer as an item (recommended if you need to provide credentials to access the service) or directly to a map. To do this, you need to copy the roads layer's URL from the REST endpoint of the map service.\nL54: The URL for a map service's REST endpoint is in the format https://<server>.<domain>/server/rest/services/<service_name>/MapServer. A link for each layer in the service is listed in the Layers section of this page. Click the link for the layer you want to use. Copy the URL of the page that opens to obtain the layer's URL.\nL55: ##### Note:\nL56: \nL57: Feature layers created from a layer in a map service are read-only; you cannot enable editing on them.\nL58: \nL59: ## Feature collections\nL60: \nL61: Feature collection items are a subset of feature layers with focused and limited functionality. They are derived from other data sources.\nL62: \nL63: Feature collections can also be exported from hosted feature layers that allow data exports. However, feature collections support limited styles and cannot be used in Map Viewer.\nL64: ## Feature layer functionality\nL65: \nL66: The following sections list how to create each type of feature layer and compare available functionality with each type in cite25†Map Viewer , cite26†Scene Viewer , and the cite27†item page .\nL67: ### Publishing methods\nL68: \nL69: The following list describes how you create each type of feature layer in ArcGIS Online:\nL70:   * Hosted feature layer—Add an item to ArcGIS Online and publish, publish from ArcGIS Pro, create an empty hosted feature layer from a template or an existing feature layer, or define the schema for an empty hosted feature layer. See cite12†Publish hosted feature layers for more information.\nL71:   * ArcGIS Server feature layer—Publish from ArcGIS Pro to an ArcGIS Server site and cite23†add the service to ArcGIS Online.\nL72:   * Feature collection—cite28†Export data from a hosted feature layer to a feature collection.\nL73: ### Map Viewer\nL74: \nL75: You can add hosted feature layers and ArcGIS Server feature layers to the new Map Viewer in ArcGIS Online, but the available functionality differs slightly for each type of layer. The following table compares feature layer functionality in Map Viewer:\nL76: ####\nL77: Functionality  | Hosted feature layer  | Hosted Indoors Spaces feature layer  | ArcGIS Server feature layer\nL78: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL79: Edit (if editing is enabled on the layer)  | Yes The layer owner, organization administrators, and members of shared update groups with which the layer is shared can edit even if editing is not enabled on the layer.  | Anyone with access to the layer can edit it even if editing isn't enabled.  | Yes\nL80: cite6†Change style , cite29†set transparency , and cite30†set visible range | Yes  | Yes  | Yes\nL81: Define labels  | Yes  | Yes  | Yes\nL82: cite31†Configure pop-ups | Yes  | Yes  | Yes\nL83: Use cite32†feature analysis tools | Yes  | Yes  | Yes\nL84: cite33†Duplicate and save layer in a map  | Yes  | Yes  | Yes\nL85: Create cite34†group layers | Yes  | Yes  | Yes\nL86: cite35†Configure forms for editable layers  | Yes  | Yes  | Yes\nL87: cite36†Apply filters | Yes  | Yes  | Yes\nL88: Calculate fields in the table  | Yes  | Yes  | No\nL89: Add fields to and delete fields from the table  | Yes  | Yes  | No\nL90: ### Scene Viewer\nL91: \nL92: You can cite37†add hosted feature layers and ArcGIS Server feature layers to Scene Viewer . Each layer can contain an unlimited number of features.\nL93: \nL94: ### Item page\nL95: \nL96: Many of the cite38†settings and details you configure for feature layers are the same for all items. Settings and details that are different across feature layers are listed in the following table:\nL97: ####\nL98: Functionality  | Hosted feature layer (including Indoors Spaces feature layer)  | ArcGIS Server feature layer  | Feature collection\nL99: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL100: Publish other layers from  | Yes  | No  | Yes, a hosted feature layer\nL101: cite15†Create views from  | Yes  | You cannot create views from a feature layer in ArcGIS Online, but the equivalent workflow is to publish multiple feature layers from the same data in an ArcGIS Desktop app or ArcGIS Pro.  | No\nL102: cite39†Define view areas on the Visualization tab  | Yes  | Not applicable  | Not applicable\nL103: cite28†Export data from  | Yes  | No  | No\nL104: Add and view cite40†metadata | Yes  | Yes  | Yes\nL105: cite41†Enable attachments | Yes  | You cannot enable attachments in ArcGIS Online. However, you can enable attachments on the source data before publishing the feature layer.  | No\nL106: cite42†Overwrite the layer | Yes  | You cannot overwrite the feature layer from ArcGIS Online. However, you can overwrite from ArcGIS Pro.  | No\nL107: cite43†Append data | Yes  | You cannot append from ArcGIS Online. However, since the data resides in the ArcGIS Server site's registered data store, append to the source data.  | No\nL108: cite44†Calculate field contents on the Data tab  | Yes  | No  | No\nL109: cite45†Add or delete fields on the Data tab  | Yes  | No  | No\nL110: cite46†Enable or disable unique constraint on columns  | Yes  | No  | No\nL111: cite47†Define domain lists and ranges | Yes  | No; you can view existing lists and ranges but cannot add or delete them on the Data tab.  | No\nL112: cite48†Add field descriptions and value types | Yes  | No  | No\nL113: Edit nonspatial field values on the Data tab  | Yes  | Yes  | No\nL114: cite49†Alter field properties including display name (title), description, field value type, unique value constraints, and whether edits are allowed for a field  | Yes  | No  | Can edit title, description, and field value type but not the other field properties.\nL115: Apply filters on the Visualization tab  | Yes  | Yes  | No\nL116: cite50†Modify settings for editing | Yes  | You cannot enable or modify editing settings from ArcGIS Online. Configure the editing settings when you publish the feature layer or modify the settings in ArcGIS Server Manager after publishing.  | No\nL117: cite51†Enable sync for offline use and collaboration  | Yes  | You cannot enable sync from ArcGIS Online. Set when publishing or modify in ArcGIS Server Manager.  | No**Sketch layers and map notes layers saved as part of a web map are automatically enabled for offline use. Other feature collection layers or layer items do not support offline use.\nL118: cite52†Track edits | Yes  | You cannot enable editor tracking from ArcGIS Online. However, you can enable editor tracking on the source data from an ArcGIS Desktop app or ArcGIS Pro.  | No\nL119: Add and manage indexes  | Yes  | No; define indexes in the source database.  | No\nL120: Update details, data, and visualization configuration for individual sublayers  | Yes  | Yes  | No\nL121: * * *\nL122: \nL123: cite53†Feedback on this topic? L124: ##### In this topic\nL125: \nL126:   1. cite1†Layers in feature layers L127:   2. cite2†Hosted feature layers L128:   3. cite3†ArcGIS Server feature layers L129:   4. cite4†Feature collections L130:   5. cite5†Feature layer functionality L131: \nL132: cite54†flist "
